former chief of the general staff  general lord danette joins me now  and certainly this was the  commander-in-chief of all armed  personnel who swore an oath of  allegiance to her what will their loss  be felt today  well um  the loss is very real and is very great  i think as we all woke up this morning  we had that sort of sinking realization  in our hearts that the day that we had  dreaded that the queen would die was  yesterday and today is the new day it's  beginning of something else and you're  absolutely right for members of the  armed forces the royal navy the british  army of the royal air force  it's particularly special it's also very  personal because on the day that we join  one of the three armed forces we swear  an oath of allegiance and not to the  government um not to the minister of  state for defense or anything like that  but actually to the sovereign  and 28th of august 1969 when i held a  bible in my hand i swore an oath of  allegiance to  the queen queen elizabeth ii but that  allegiance as she breathed her last  breath  passed seamlessly  to the new king to king charles iii and  that's who our allegiance is with now  and you know  it's an incredible aspect part of the  unbritten british constitution for those  of us who choose to serve the nation in  uniform  we serve the nation we serve the person  of the sovereign and that's a very  special aspect and um that allegiance  has now passed uh just in a flash of  lightning as it were from queen  elizabeth ii to king charles iii that's  very very special and this is now the  start of something new just as we look  back with great fondness and all our  memories of just how special that person  of queen elizabeth ii was  and of course this is a family that's  that's steeped in military history  itself is it not you know prince philip  committed to the navy a job that he had  to give up to marry the queen prince  andrew  served in the falklands princess william  and harry to harry in afghanistan you  know it really was all around them are  the queen's own children and  grandchildren being part of this service  well that's absolutely right um  the sovereign and the members of the  royal family take their very close  association with  the royal navy the british army the  royal air force very very seriously and  and it's really important that they do  and they do it very willingly and they  do it extraordinarily well because  it's the embodiment of that personal  link between every individual service  man and service woman with  the head of state with the sovereign and  that link is is so very very special so  as i said just a moment or two ago  this is a great loss for the nation but  actually it's a very personal loss for  soldiers sailors airmen and women and  marines um because the the sovereign  is our our leader is our  commander-in-chief um  and that  commander-in-chief role passed  seamlessly yesterday from queen  elizabeth ii to king charles iii it's  very special it's very personal i think  it's almost unique in the world  other countries like the united states  and france  the elected politician who is the head  of state um  is there a  senior person but in this country there  is something mystical there's something  magical there's something spiritual  there's something very special about  having the sovereign as the head of  state and the head of our armed forces  um  i think it's unique it's certainly very  very special  well we hear you certainly law richard  dannett former chief of the general  staff thank you  thank you
 

